#* {{quote-book|en|author=Hubert Cole|chapter=King Henry I|title=Christophe: King of Haiti|location=London|publisher=w:Eyre & Spottiswoode|year=1967|page=208|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/christophekingof0000hube/page/208/mode/1up|passage=Princes and princesses, '''ducs''' and duchesses were privileged to seat themselves on the traditional ''tabourets''; comtes and comtesses, barons and baronnes, chevaliers and chevalières made do with the lesser ''pliants''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:R. W. B. Lewis|R[ichard] W[arrington] B[aldwin] Lewis]]|chapter=Within the Tide|title=Edith Wharton: A Biography|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper Colophon]]|year=1977|section=section V (The War Years: 1913–1918)|page=401|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/edithwhartonbiog0000lewi_c1c5/page/401/mode/1up|isbn=0-06-090554-9|passage=After becoming the familiar of the '''ducs''' and duchesses, the comtes and comtesses he would draw upon, splice, and reshape for his epic novel, he withdrew abruptly following the second trial of Alfred Dreyfus in 1899.|footer={{small|The [https://archive.org/details/edithwhartonbiog0000lewi_m5r3/page/401/mode/1up 1975 edition] uses italics.}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Elizabeth Lambert|Penelope Williamson]]|title=Hearts Beguiled|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Avon (publisher)|Avon Books]]|year=1989|page=349|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/heartsbeguiled0000will/page/349/mode/1up|isbn=0-380-75600-5|passage=Marie-Rose had once paid fifty livres she could ill afford to have a dancing master teach Gabrielle all the various bows and curtsies decreed by court etiquette. There was one kind of curtsy for the king and queen, another for princes and princesses of the blood, one for '''ducs''' and duchesses, and still another for lesser mortals.}}
