#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Darwin Porter; Danforth Prince|title=[[w:Frommer's|Frommer’s]] France ’99|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Macmillan Publishers|Macmillan Travel]]|year=1999|page=251|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=WZIA2QICr74C&q=%22ducs%20and%22|isbn=0-02-862276-6|issn=0899-3351|passage=Orléans is the chief town of Loiret, on the Loire, and beneficiary of countless associations with the French aristocracy—even giving its name to '''ducs''' and duchesses who influenced the course of the nation’s history.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Mary Gentle|title=The Wild Machines|series=[[w:w:Ash: A Secret History|The Book of Ash]]|seriesvolume=3|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Eos, {{w|HarperCollins}}|year=2000|page=11|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/wildmachines0000gent/page/11/mode/1up|isbn=0-380-81113-8|passage=It is not recorded that any of the French '''ducs''' reacted to this incursion on their territory.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Patricia Beard|chapter=A Bachelor Abroad, 1899|title=After the Ball: Gilded Age Secrets, Boardroom Betrayals, and the Party That Ignited the Great Wall Street Scandal of 1905|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:HarperCollins|year=2003|section=part two (The Richest Young Man in New York)|page=70|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/afterballgildeda0000bear/page/70/mode/1up|isbn=0-06-019939-3|passage=There were marquises and vicomtesses and comtesses, '''ducs''' and duchesses, two Russian grand dukes, Marcel Proust, and the most prominent courtesans in Paris.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Linda Lee Chaikin|chapter=2|title=Daughter of Silk|series=The Silk House Series|location=Grand Rapids, Mich.|publisher=w:Zondervan|year=2006|page=35|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=X5uho7WGpwgC&pg=PA35|isbn=978-0-310-26300-5|passage=The court, consisting of '''ducs''' and duchesses, comtes and comtesses, had assembled here at Chambord for several months of entertainment.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Charles Gibson|chapter=In Old Provence|title=Little Pilgrimages Among French Inns|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Cosimo, Inc.|year=2006|page=385|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=q5a7CVEpG64C&pg=PA385|isbn=1-59605-141-8|passage=The Duc d’Uzès became the first of the French '''ducs''' under Louis XIII., taking precedence of the Duc de Luynes, on account of arriving first at the palace, having, according to the historical anecdote, overturned the latter’s carriage in his haste to be first before the king to verify his title.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Veronica Buckley|chapter=Crusaders|title=Madame de Maintenon: The Secret Wife of Louis XIV|location=London|publisher=w:Bloomsbury Publishing|year=2008|page=326|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/madamedemainteno0000buck/page/326/mode/1up|isbn=978 0 7475 8098 0|passage=‘No one knows how this ''for'' distinction came about,’ continued Saint-Simon, ‘and really it’s idiotic. It just means that you have ''for So-and-so'' chalked on the door of your room, instead of just ''So-and-so''. Princes of the blood, cardinals, and foreign princes all get a ''for'', and some '''ducs''' and duchesses have got them, but it doesn’t mean your room will be any better than anyone else’s, and that’s why I think it’s idiotic .&nbsp;.&nbsp;.’ concluded the '''duc''', himself notoriously niggly about protocol, and incidentally ''for''-less.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Donna Russo Morin|chapter=17|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=t6-ABlGfxQkC&pg=PT225|title=The Courtier’s Secret|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Kensington Publishing|year=2009|isbn=0-7582-3982-3|passage=The mass ended, and as the courtiers filed out in proper order she noticed Madame La Marechal, Lynette’s mother, still on her knees in the pew, though the line of '''ducs''' and duchesses had already passed from the room.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Ardis Butterfield|chapter=[Mother Tongues: English and French in fifteenth-century England] Agincourt and its effects|title=The Familiar Enemy: Chaucer, Language and Nation in the Hundred Years War|location=Oxford, Oxon|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|year=2009|section=part III (Vernacular Subjects)|page=314|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/familiarenemycha0000butt/page/314/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-19-957486-5|passage=Henry had made great gains on a variety of fronts: negotiating artfully between the rival French '''ducs''' of Bourgogne and Orléans, and the mentally unstable Valois king, Charles VI, he had seized the opportunities created by their bitter feuds.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Juliet Grey|chapter=''Rien''|title=Becoming Marie Antoinette|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Ballantine Books|year=2011|page=241|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=SS5b6NixVOcC&pg=PA241|isbn=978-0-345-52386-0|passage=There, like the monkeys in my father’s zoo at the palace of Laxenburg, whose antics were displayed for the delight of the Austrian elite, Louis Auguste and I were expected to amuse the crush of '''ducs''' and duchesses, marquis and marquises, and comtes and comtesses whose rank accorded them the privilege of watching the heirs to the throne of France play a few rounds of cavagnole with members of the Orléans branch of the Bourbons.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Alan Gold (author)|Alan Gold]]|chapter=12|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=QLJGCgAAQBAJ&pg=PT249|title=The Pretender’s Lady|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Yucca Publishing, {{w|Skyhorse Publishing}}|year=2015|isbn=978-1-63158-048-2|passage=Here, resplendent in their uniforms of office, were the princes and princesses, the '''ducs''' and duchesses, the marquises and marchionesses, the maréchals and the generals of the French Army, the prelates of the Holy Roman Church and their mistresses, sycophants and influence peddlers, deposed members of foreign royalty, aged courtiers of Louis the Sun King, current and past court officials, members of the Académie Royale, scientists, philosophers, artists, courtesans, past and present mistresses of the king as well as lesser family and irrelevant nobility from the provinces, all assembled at the glittering court of King Louis XV like overlooked and faded ''objets d’art'' in an{{sic}} storeroom.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Belinda Scerri|chapter=‘Instructing herself by fad or fancy’: Depictions and Fictions of ''Connoisseuses'' and ''Femmes Savantes'' in Eighteenth-Century Paris|editors=Beatrijs Vanacker; Lieke van Deinsen|title=Portraits and Poses: Female Intellectual Authority, Agency and Authorship in Early Modern Europe|location=Leuven|publisher=w:Leuven University Press|year=2022|section=part II (Types and Models of Female Intellectual Authority)|page=187|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6MhcEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA187|isbn=978 94 6270 330 8|passage=She was the eighth child of the '''duc''' and duchesse of Enghien and, as a member of the reigning Bourbon house, was styled a ''princesse du sang'', or princess of the blood.}}

